Introduction to Microsoft Defender XDR Threat Protection

Date: October 3, 2026
Platform / Environment: Microsoft Learn — Microsoft Defender XDR

What I Did

Completed the Microsoft Learn module Introduction to Microsoft Defender XDR threat protection.

The module introduced Microsoft’s integrated Extended Detection and Response (XDR) approach and how Defender XDR can be used within a Security Operations Center (SOC).

What I Learned

I learned about:

* Extended Detection and Response (XDR) use cases
* The role of Microsoft Defender XDR within a SOC
* Microsoft Security Graph
* Investigating security incidents in Defender XDR
* How security signals can be brought together to support threat investigation and response

Key Takeaway

One of the main concepts I took from the module is that modern security monitoring isn’t just about looking at individual alerts.

XDR can help connect security signals across different areas of an environment, giving security teams more context when investigating an incident.

This can make it easier to understand whether separate alerts are related to the same attack rather than treating every alert as an isolated event.

Practical Relevance

This builds on my previous learning around Microsoft Defender for Endpoint.

Understanding the wider Defender XDR ecosystem gives me a better foundation for understanding how endpoint detection fits into a broader security operations environment.

Next Step

Continue building my Microsoft security knowledge and get hands-on experience with Defender tools and security investigation workflows where possible.
